# AmberEdit keyboard layout — the defaults, written out.
#
# Point at it with a "keys" line in amberedit.cfg:
#
#   keys ~/ftn/etc/amberkeys.cfg
#
# A config naming no file at all gets exactly this layout, so there is nothing
# to copy unless something is to change.
#
# THE FILE IS THE LAYOUT ENTIRE. A command no line here names has no key at all
# — this is a layout rather than a list of corrections, so copy the whole of it
# and edit what you want moved. A command may be named on several lines, and it
# then answers to each of those keys.
#
# A line is a key and a command, in that order:
#
#   l    reader.list
#   F2   reader.change
#
# Blank lines are ignored, and a line starting with "#" is a comment — which is
# why "#" itself is the one printable character that cannot be a key here.
#
# Keys are written as:
#
#   a single character   l  G  /  +        upper and lower case are different keys
#   a function key       F1 ... F12
#   Del
#   Ctrl-<letter>        Ctrl-N            case does not matter after Ctrl-
#   Alt-<letter>         Alt-F
#   Alt-<arrow>          Alt-Left  Alt-Right  Alt-Up  Alt-Down
#
# Moving about cannot be rebound, and naming one of those keys here is an error
# rather than a line that is quietly dropped: the arrows, PgUp and PgDn, Home and
# End, Space, Enter, Esc, Backspace and Tab mean the same thing on every screen
# and in every dialog, and a layout that had taken Esc away would leave you on a
# screen with no way out of it. The dialogs answer for themselves entirely — what
# Enter, Esc and the arrows do in a box is the box's, and so are the attribute
# chords.
#
# Two screens may share a key: F2 below is Change in the reader and Save in the
# editor, and neither screen is ever the other. Two commands of one screen may
# not, and a key written twice for one screen is an error naming both.
#
# A bare letter bound in the area list stops being a letter the quick search can
# be typed with — the commands are answered first. By default only "/" is taken
# there, and no area tag holds one.

# --- anywhere -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Quitting, from whatever screen is up — except the attributes dialog, where
# every chord belongs to the dialog while it is on the screen.
Ctrl-Q          app.quit

# --- the area list ------------------------------------------------------------
/               arealist.next-unread
Ctrl-R          arealist.rescan

# --- the reader ---------------------------------------------------------------
q               reader.reply
F4              reader.reply
n               reader.reply-elsewhere
F5              reader.reply-elsewhere
Alt-Q           reader.comment-reply
e               reader.new
m               reader.forward
c               reader.change
F2              reader.change
d               reader.delete
Del             reader.delete
w               reader.export
F7              reader.export
Ctrl-F          reader.find
F6              reader.find
l               reader.list
F9              reader.list
i               reader.info
Ctrl-N          reader.nodelist
F10             reader.nodelist
k               reader.kludges
b               reader.scrollbar
-               reader.thread-up
+               reader.thread-down
=               reader.thread-down

# --- the editor ---------------------------------------------------------------
Ctrl-S          compose.save
F2              compose.save
Ctrl-F          compose.attributes
Ctrl-O          compose.import
Alt-H           compose.header-back
Ctrl-Y          compose.delete-line
Ctrl-U          compose.restore-line
Ctrl-D          compose.delete-quote
Ctrl-W          compose.delete-word
Alt-Backspace   compose.delete-word
Alt-B           compose.word-left
Alt-Left        compose.word-left
Alt-F           compose.word-right
Alt-Right       compose.word-right
Ctrl-A          compose.line-start
Ctrl-E          compose.line-end
